# Tool Input Validation

| Field | Value |
| ----- | ----- |
| Issue | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
| Status | Draft |

The registry owns validation of model-supplied tool input against the same JSON Schema advertised to providers.

## Execution contract

- Before invoking an executor, "'`execute` rejects missing required fields, wrong declared types and invalid enum members with a descriptive `ToolResponse.result`, naming property paths and constraints without exposing input values in returned text or logs; valid input, including unknown keys, reaches the executor exactly once without coercion or defaults, and an unknown tool name still throws `Unknown tool: <name>`.
- Static schemas compile at registration, with invalid schemas throwing an error naming the tool, and a successfully compiled schema is reused across ten executions.
- Function-valued definitions are resolved for each execution, so changing the returned schema changes which inputs are accepted, while reusing a resolved schema object reuses its compiled validator.

## Integration and delivery

Rejections are tool results available to the model on its next round; the existing five-round default bounds correction attempts. The implementation must retain the existing tool-loop contract and document it in README.md, docs/adding-a-tool.md and .specify/spec.md. The validator ships as a runtime dependency. The executor'"'s one-line doc comment points here. Release work includes a consumer-facing changelog, a MINOR version bump and a migration note explaining that executors no longer receive schema-invalid inputs for custom error handling.
